<p>President Trump called off a planned trip to Islamabad by special envoy Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner that was meant to pursue talks with Iran on ending the nearly eight-week war. Trump said he canceled the trip because too much time would be wasted on travel and because of what he described as confusion inside Iran’s leadership. He also said the move did not mean the United States had decided to resume fighting with Iran. Pakistan had quickly assembled the talks as an intermediary, but the opening narrowed after Iran said it had no plans for a direct meeting with the U.S. delegation and Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi left Islamabad after meeting Pakistani leaders. Even so, some channels stayed open: Trump said Iran could still reach out, Araghchi called his Pakistan visit fruitful, and Iranian state media said he was expected to return to Islamabad after meetings in Oman — a small sign that mediation efforts had not fully closed.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/mNBp6K_TZhT7zTZXlaipljC0dMmzJnvXVXniN2FRyEtEFHiXgbTmjnudq8SDH_hFWfZQDnx3G4Vis5zO9Z8Gi_YseGgx3NoU_SftqY4dFFOKv3XFLHvG8g65U48hpvtvsWXhcAcQLM4un5twRSqlV9XHGGp1hTyiDJHsZGFVzUa7LoMRp-JiVwHuW5Uy35KCvpB7yKOhFbwVF53in3u2J7utHbKVF48X0Iyp9XsAOE0J5UUP2EW8pWb2qA' alt='Vice President JD Vance speaks after meeting representatives from Pakistan and Iran in Islamabad on April 12, 2026, as Jared Kushner and Steve Witkoff listen.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Iranian stance: Iran said it would not accept what it called “maximalist” U.S. demands, and Araghchi said Tehran was still waiting to see whether Washington was truly serious about diplomacy.</li><li>Regional movement: After leaving Islamabad, Araghchi arrived in Muscat for meetings with Omani officials, showing regional mediators were still engaged despite the setback in Pakistan.</li><li>Ceasefire strain: Several outlets described the diplomatic pause as a setback for hopes of a ceasefire breakthrough as the conflict entered day 58 and the two sides remained far apart on terms.</li><li>Flights resume: Iran resumed commercial flights from Tehran’s international airport for the first time since the conflict began, a modest sign of partial normalization even as negotiations stalled.</li></ul><blockquote>Too much time wasted on traveling, too much work! Besides which, there is tremendous infighting and confusion within their ‘leadership’. Nobody knows who is in charge, including them. Also, we have all the cards, they have none! If they want to talk, all they have to do is call!!! - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>United States: President Trump said he canceled the trip because he did not want his envoys making a long flight only to “sit around talking about nothing,” and said Tehran could contact Washington if it wanted talks. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/trump-canceled-kushner-witkoff-trip-pakistan-iran-talks/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>Iran: Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said his Pakistan visit was “very fruitful,” praised Pakistan’s efforts to restore peace, and said Iran was still waiting to see whether the U.S. was serious about diplomacy. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1994958/iranian-delegation-led-by-araghchi-leaves-after-meeting-pm-cdf-munir-trump-cancels-us-envoys-visit'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Pakistan: Pakistani officials kept hosting meetings and shuttling messages between the sides, with Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif and military leaders meeting Araghchi as Islamabad tried to keep a mediation track alive. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1994958/iranian-delegation-led-by-araghchi-leaves-after-meeting-pm-cdf-munir-trump-cancels-us-envoys-visit'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/middle-east-crisis/article/2026/04/25/trump-cancels-envoys-trip-to-pakistan-for-iran-talks_6752796_369.html'>Trump cancels envoys&#x27; trip to Pakistan for Iran talks</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c4g6